# TypeScript/JavaScript (Node.js) patterns

Idiomatic design patterns and anti-patterns for Node.js services, libraries
and CLIs written in TypeScript or JavaScript. Scoped to server/library/CLI
code; component composition patterns belong to the `react` pack.

## Idiomatic patterns

- Validate external input (HTTP body, CLI args, env vars, file content) at
  the boundary where it enters the process, with a schema library (`zod`,
  `valibot`, `ajv`) if the project already has one, or explicit checks if
  not -- do not let unvalidated `unknown` data flow deep into business
  logic and get treated as trusted.
- Use dependency injection (constructor/factory parameters) for anything
  that talks to the outside world (DB client, HTTP client, clock, RNG) so
  it can be swapped for a test double -- do not reach for a global
  singleton in code that needs to be testable.
- Prefer small, composable functions over a class hierarchy when there is
  no shared mutable state to encapsulate; reach for a class specifically
  when you need to hold state across calls (a connection pool, a cache).
- Use `Array.prototype` methods (`map`/`filter`/`reduce`/`flatMap`) for
  data transforms instead of manual index loops with push, unless a hot
  loop's profiling shows the allocation cost matters.
- Return a discriminated union (`{ ok: true, value } | { ok: false, error
  }`) or throw a typed error class for an operation with an expected
  failure mode -- do not overload a return value's meaning with `null`/
  `undefined`/`-1` for both "not found" and "failed".
- Use `EventEmitter`/`AsyncIterator` for a genuine stream of events over
  time; do not reach for either to model a single async result -- that is
  what a `Promise` is for.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- **God module / barrel re-export cycle**: an `index.ts` that re-exports
  everything from a package and gets imported back into one of the modules
  it re-exports -- creates a circular dependency the bundler/`tsc` may
  tolerate but that breaks initialization order at runtime.
- **Synchronous work in an async-looking function**: an `async function`
  whose body is entirely synchronous CPU work with no `await` -- it still
  blocks the event loop exactly like a non-async function; the `async`
  keyword adds a microtask, not a thread.
- **Manual promise construction around an already-async API**: wrapping a
  callback-based Node API in `new Promise(...)` when `node:util`'s
  `promisify` or the API's own `.promises`/`fs/promises` variant already
  exists -- duplicates logic the platform maintains for you.
- **Mutating a shared config/options object**: a function that receives an
  options object and mutates it in place instead of returning a new one --
  surprises every other caller holding a reference to the same object.
- **Deep relative import chains** (`../../../../lib/x`) reaching across
  module boundaries instead of a package-level export -- signals the
  module boundary itself is wrong, not that the import needs a path alias.
- **try/catch that only rethrows the same error unchanged**: adds a stack
  frame and nothing else; either add context (`cause`, a wrapped error
  type) or remove the try/catch.
